(window.DETAIL_C = window.DETAIL_C || {})["mth-12-10"] = {"mth-12-10-m01":{"idea":"Raise the power by one, then divide by the new power.","steps":["Add 1 to the power: n becomes n + 1.","Divide by the new power n + 1.","Add the constant c. Check by differentiating, which gives back xⁿ."],"others":"x^(n+1) without dividing is wrong because the derivative would carry a factor n + 1. n·x^(n−1) is the derivative, not the integral. x^(n−1)/(n−1) lowers the power instead of raising it.","trap":"Integration raises the power. The case n = −1 is different.","answer":"x^(n+1)/(n+1) + c"},"mth-12-10-m02":{"idea":"The derivative of log x is 1/x, so log x is the antiderivative.","steps":["The power rule fails for 1/x, since it needs n = −1.","d/dx log x = 1/x, so the integral is log x.","For any x not equal to 0, the full answer is log|x| + c."],"others":"1/x² and −1/x² are derivatives of other functions. x log x differentiates to log x + 1, not 1/x.","trap":"1/x is the one power that gives a log.","answer":"log x + c"},"mth-12-10-m03":{"idea":"eˣ is its own derivative, so it is its own antiderivative.","steps":["d/dx eˣ = eˣ.","So the integral of eˣ is eˣ.","Do not forget the constant c."],"others":"eˣ log x and eˣ/(x + 1) do not differentiate back to eˣ. x eˣ differentiates to eˣ + x eˣ.","trap":"No change except + c.","answer":"eˣ + c"},"mth-12-10-m04":{"idea":"Find a function whose derivative is sin x.","steps":["The derivative of cos x is −sin x.","So the derivative of −cos x is +sin x.","The integral is −cos x + c."],"others":"cos x + c differentiates to −sin x. −sin x + c differentiates to −cos x. tan x + c differentiates to sec²x.","trap":"Watch the minus sign.","answer":"−cos x + c"},"mth-12-10-m05":{"idea":"Match sec²x to a known derivative.","steps":["The derivative of tan x is sec²x.","So the integral of sec²x is tan x.","Add the constant c at the end."],"others":"−cot x differentiates to cosec²x. sec x tan x is the derivative of sec x. sec x is the antiderivative of sec x tan x, not of sec²x.","trap":"Do not mix sec x tan x and sec²x.","answer":"tan x + c"},"mth-12-10-m06":{"idea":"Constants disappear on differentiating, so any constant can be added back.","steps":["If F′(x) = f(x), then (F + c)′ = f(x) for every constant c.","So the integral is a whole family of curves, each shifted up or down.","A condition like F(1) = 2 picks one of them."],"others":"The constant is not there to fix the answer or for accuracy. Integration is exact. Area under a curve is a definite integral, which has no constant.","trap":"c comes from the zero derivative of a constant.","answer":"constants have zero derivative, so any constant can be added to an antiderivative"},"mth-12-10-m07":{"idea":"Integrate each term separately.","steps":["The integral of 3x² is x³.","The integral of 2x is x².","So the answer is x³ + x² + c. Differentiate to check."],"others":"x³ + x leaves the second term wrong. 6x + 2 is the derivative. 3x³ + 2x² multiplies by the power instead of dividing.","trap":"Divide by the new power.","answer":"x³ + x² + c"},"mth-12-10-m08":{"idea":"Write 1/x² as x⁻² and use the power rule.","steps":["1/x² = x⁻².","Raise the power: x⁻¹, and divide by −1.","That is −1/x. The log rule is only for the power −1."],"others":"1/x has the wrong sign. log x² would be for 1/x. −2/x³ is the derivative of 1/x².","trap":"Negative power, so the sign flips.","answer":"−1/x + c"},"mth-12-10-m09":{"idea":"Inside the cosine is 3x, so divide by 3.","steps":["The derivative of sin 3x is 3 cos 3x.","So to get cos 3x, divide by 3.","The integral is (1/3) sin 3x + c."],"others":"sin 3x without dividing differentiates to 3 cos 3x. 3 sin 3x multiplies instead of dividing. −(1/3) sin 3x has a wrong sign.","trap":"Divide by the coefficient of x.","answer":"(1/3) sin 3x + c"},"mth-12-10-m10":{"idea":"Substitute t = 2x.","steps":["Put t = 2x, so dx = dt/2.","The integral becomes the integral of eᵗ dt/2, which is eᵗ/2.","Back in x: e^(2x)/2 + c."],"others":"e^(2x)/(2x) divides by the whole exponent, which is wrong. 2e^(2x) multiplies instead of dividing. e^(2x) alone leaves out the factor 1/2.","trap":"Divide by the coefficient, not by 2x.","answer":"e^(2x)/2 + c"},"mth-12-10-m11":{"idea":"Substitute t = 2x + 3.","steps":["Put t = 2x + 3, so dx = dt/2.","The integral becomes the integral of dt/(2t), which is (1/2) log|t|.","Back in x: (1/2) log|2x + 3| + c."],"others":"The squared form is the derivative of a different function. log|2x + 3| leaves out the factor 1/2. 2 log|2x + 3| multiplies instead of dividing.","trap":"The coefficient of x goes into the denominator.","answer":"(1/2) log|2x + 3| + c"},"mth-12-10-m12":{"idea":"x dx is half of the derivative of x², so substitute t = x².","steps":["Put t = x², so 2x dx = dt and x dx = dt/2.","The integral becomes the integral of eᵗ dt/2, which is eᵗ/2.","Back in x: (1/2) e^(x²) + c."],"others":"2 e^(x²) multiplies by 2 instead of halving. x² e^(x²)/2 treats the factor x wrongly. e^(x²) alone leaves out 1/2.","trap":"Match x dx with dt/2.","answer":"(1/2) e^(x²) + c"},"mth-12-10-m13":{"idea":"Write tan x as sin x over cos x and substitute t = cos x.","steps":["tan x = sin x/cos x. Put t = cos x, so dt = −sin x dx.","The integral becomes the integral of −dt/t, which is −log|t|.","−log|cos x| = log|sec x|."],"others":"−log|sec x| is the integral of −tan x, which has the wrong sign. sec²x is the derivative of tan x. log|sin x| is the integral of cot x.","trap":"cos in the denominator brings a minus sign.","answer":"log|sec x| + c"},"mth-12-10-m14":{"idea":"Use the standard form with a² = 9.","steps":["The standard result is the integral of dx/(x² + a²) = (1/a) tan⁻¹(x/a) + c.","Here a = 3.","So the answer is (1/3) tan⁻¹(x/3) + c."],"others":"Leaving out 1/3 gives a derivative that is 3 times too large. 3 tan⁻¹(x/3) multiplies instead of dividing. (1/9) tan⁻¹(x/9) uses 9 for a instead of 3.","trap":"a is the square root of 9.","answer":"(1/3) tan⁻¹(x/3) + c"},"mth-12-10-m15":{"idea":"Use the standard form with a² = 16.","steps":["The standard result is the integral of dx/√(a² − x²) = sin⁻¹(x/a) + c.","Here a = 4.","So the answer is sin⁻¹(x/4) + c, with no extra 1/a factor."],"others":"cos⁻¹(x/4) would have the opposite sign. sin⁻¹ x forgets the 4. (1/4) sin⁻¹(x/4) borrows the factor from the tan⁻¹ formula.","trap":"Only the tan⁻¹ formula has the factor 1/a.","answer":"sin⁻¹(x/4) + c"},"mth-12-10-m16":{"idea":"There is no product rule for integrals.","steps":["Sum rule and constant factor rule do hold.","Two antiderivatives of the same function differ by a constant.","But the integral of a product is not the product of integrals: ∫x·x dx = x³/3, while (∫x dx)(∫x dx) = x⁴/4."],"others":"The other three statements are true rules of integration.","trap":"The false statement is the product one.","answer":"the integral of a product is the product of the integrals"},"mth-12-10-m17":{"idea":"Use the standard form for a difference of squares.","steps":["The standard result is the integral of dx/(x² − a²) = (1/2a) log|(x − a)/(x + a)| + c.","With a = 2, 1/(2a) = 1/4.","So the answer is (1/4) log|(x − 2)/(x + 2)| + c."],"others":"tan⁻¹ belongs to x² + a², not x² − a². Swapping the ratio flips the sign. A factor 1/2 forgets that 2a = 4.","trap":"Minus sign gives log, plus sign gives tan⁻¹.","answer":"(1/4) log|(x − 2)/(x + 2)| + c"},"mth-12-10-m18":{"idea":"Integrate by parts, with log x as the first function.","steps":["By LIATE, take u = log x and v = x.","∫x log x dx = (x²/2) log x − ∫(1/x)(x²/2) dx.","The last integral is x²/4, so the answer is (x²/2) log x − x²/4 + c."],"others":"Subtracting x²/2 forgets the factor 1/2 that comes from the second integral. Subtracting x/2 uses the wrong power. Adding x²/4 has the wrong sign.","trap":"Second integral is ∫x/2 dx.","answer":"(x²/2) log x − x²/4 + c"},"mth-12-10-m19":{"idea":"Integrate by parts with u = x.","steps":["Take u = x and v = eˣ.","∫x eˣ dx = x eˣ − ∫eˣ dx = x eˣ − eˣ.","That equals (x − 1)eˣ + c. Differentiate to check."],"others":"(x + 1)eˣ differentiates to (x + 2)eˣ. x eˣ alone differentiates to eˣ + x eˣ. (x²/2) eˣ is not correct either.","trap":"Minus eˣ, so x − 1.","answer":"(x − 1)eˣ + c"},"mth-12-10-m20":{"idea":"Spot that eˣ f(x) differentiates to the integrand.","steps":["Differentiate eˣ f(x): eˣ f(x) + eˣ f′(x).","That is exactly eˣ[f(x) + f′(x)].","So the integral is eˣ f(x) + c."],"others":"A product of f and f′ is not what appears. A minus sign would need eˣ[f − f′]. eˣ f′(x) differentiates to something different.","trap":"Function plus its derivative.","answer":"eˣ f(x) + c"},"mth-12-10-m21":{"idea":"cos x is the derivative of sin x, so this has the form eˣ[f + f′].","steps":["Take f(x) = sin x, so f′(x) = cos x.","The integrand is eˣ[f + f′].","The integral is eˣ f(x) = eˣ sin x + c."],"others":"eˣ(sin x − cos x) is the integral of −2eˣ cos x plus something else. eˣ cos x differentiates to eˣ(cos x − sin x). −eˣ sin x has the wrong sign.","trap":"Choose f as the first term.","answer":"eˣ sin x + c"},"mth-12-10-m22":{"idea":"The numerator is the derivative of the denominator.","steps":["Put t = x² + 3x + 5, so dt = (2x + 3) dx.","The integral becomes the integral of dt/t, which is log t.","No modulus is needed, because the quadratic is always positive."],"others":"tan⁻¹ of the quadratic is not the rule here. 2 log(...) has an extra factor. log(2x + 3) uses only the numerator.","trap":"Derivative on top gives log of the bottom.","answer":"log(x² + 3x + 5) + c"},"mth-12-10-m23":{"idea":"Use the cover-up method on 1 = A(x − 2) + B(x − 1).","steps":["Multiply both sides by (x − 1)(x − 2).","Put x = 1: 1 = −A, so A = −1.","Put x = 2: 1 = B. So A = −1 and B = 1."],"others":"Both negative or both positive cannot satisfy the equation at both x = 1 and x = 2. The last option swaps the signs.","trap":"Substitute the root that kills the other term.","answer":"A = −1 and B = 1"},"mth-12-10-m24":{"idea":"Split into partial fractions.","steps":["1/(x(x + 1)) = 1/x − 1/(x + 1).","Integrate: log|x| − log|x + 1|.","Combine: log|x/(x + 1)| + c."],"others":"−1/(x(x + 1)) is not an antiderivative. log|x(x + 1)| adds the logs instead of subtracting. The flipped ratio has the wrong sign.","trap":"Minus between the logs, so a quotient.","answer":"log|x/(x + 1)| + c"},"mth-12-10-m25":{"idea":"Substitute t = log x.","steps":["Put t = log x, so dt = dx/x.","The integral becomes the integral of t² dt = t³/3.","Back in x: (log x)³/3 + c."],"others":"Dividing by 3x keeps an extra 1/x. Leaving out the 1/3 is wrong. (log x)²/2 comes from integrating log x/x.","trap":"1/x is used up in dt.","answer":"(log x)³/3 + c"},"mth-12-10-m26":{"idea":"Substitute t = log x.","steps":["Put t = log x, so dt = dx/x.","The integral becomes the integral of dt/t = log|t|.","So the answer is log|log x| + c."],"others":"log x alone is the integral of 1/x. (log x)²/2 is the integral of log x/x. −1/(log x)² is the derivative of 1/log x.","trap":"A log of a log is correct here.","answer":"log|log x| + c"},"mth-12-10-n01":{"idea":"Integrate f′ and use f(0) = 3 to find the constant.","steps":["f(x) = 2x³ + 2x² + c.","f(0) = c = 3.","f(2) = 16 + 8 + 3 = 27."],"trap":"Find c before substituting x = 2.","answer":"27"},"mth-12-10-n02":{"idea":"Integrate, then fix the constant from f(1) = 5.","steps":["f(x) = x⁴ − x³ + 2x + c.","At x = 1: 1 − 1 + 2 + c = 5, so c = 3.","f(2) = 16 − 8 + 4 + 3 = 15."],"trap":"Use the given point for c.","answer":"15"},"mth-12-10-n03":{"idea":"Integrate e^(2x), then fix the constant.","steps":["f(x) = e^(2x)/2 + c.","f(0) = 0.5 + c = 1.5, so c = 1.","f(1) = e²/2 + 1 = 3.6945 + 1 = 4.695."],"trap":"Divide by 2 when integrating.","answer":"4.695"},"mth-12-10-n04":{"idea":"The slope is the derivative, so integrate it.","steps":["y = x³ − 2x + c.","At the point (1, 2): 1 − 2 + c = 2, so c = 3.","At x = 2: y = 8 − 4 + 3 = 7."],"trap":"Put the point in first to get c.","answer":"7"},"mth-12-10-n05":{"idea":"Integrate acceleration to get velocity, then velocity to get position.","steps":["v = 3t² − 4t + c1, and v(0) = 3 gives c1 = 3.","s = t³ − 2t² + 3t + c2, and s(0) = 0 gives c2 = 0.","s(2) = 8 − 8 + 6 = 6 m."],"trap":"Two integrations need two constants.","answer":"6"},"mth-12-10-n06":{"idea":"Use the by parts result for x eˣ, then fix the constant.","steps":["∫x eˣ dx = (x − 1)eˣ + c.","F(0) = −1 + c = 4, so c = 5.","F(1) = 0 + 5 = 5."],"trap":"At x = 1 the bracket (x − 1) is zero.","answer":"5"},"mth-12-10-n07":{"idea":"Use the standard form for 1/(x² + a²).","steps":["F(x) = (1/2) tan⁻¹(x/2) + c, and F(0) = 1 gives c = 1.","F(2) = (1/2) tan⁻¹ 1 + 1 = (1/2)(π/4) + 1.","That is 1 + π/8 = 1.393."],"trap":"tan⁻¹ 1 is π/4.","answer":"1.393"},"mth-12-10-n08":{"idea":"The numerator is the derivative of the denominator.","steps":["A(x) = log(x² + x) + c, and A(1) = log 2 + c = 0 gives c = −log 2.","A(3) = log 12 − log 2.","That is log 6 = 1.792."],"trap":"Subtract logs to divide.","answer":"1.792"},"mth-12-10-n09":{"idea":"Divide first, then integrate each term.","steps":["(x² + 1)/x = x + 1/x, so G(x) = x²/2 + log x + c.","G(1) = 0.5 + c = 2, so c = 1.5.","G(e) = e²/2 + 1 + 1.5 = 3.6945 + 2.5 = 6.195."],"trap":"log e = 1.","answer":"6.195"},"mth-12-10-n10":{"idea":"Use partial fractions, then fix the constant.","steps":["1/((x − 1)(x − 2)) = 1/(x − 2) − 1/(x − 1), so H(x) = log((x − 2)/(x − 1)) + c.","H(3) = log(1/2) + c = 0 gives c = log 2.","H(4) = log(2/3) + log 2 = log(4/3) = 0.288."],"trap":"On x greater than 2 both factors are positive.","answer":"0.288"}};
